   797	- <a id="decided-project-concrete-toml"></a>**Policies and declared assumptions live in
   798	  `Concrete.toml`** (owner, 2026-11-06: "concrete.toml is fine", in place of a separate policy
   799	  file). A project's policies, such as the
   800	  [predictable policy](#decided-project-predictable-policy), and its declared assumptions (target,
   801	  heap, stack, foreign functions and the trusted surface) are written in the project's
   802	  `Concrete.toml`. There is no separate policy or assumptions file.
   803	  - *Typed, versioned data.* Policy and assumption sections are typed and versioned; an unknown
   804	    section, key or version is refused, never ignored.
   805	  - *Only tightening.* A module may tighten a project policy, never relax it.
   806	  - *Dependencies.* A dependency's `Concrete.toml` never relaxes the consumer's policy: dependencies
   807	    are checked against the consumer's policy, transitively and fail-closed.
   808	  - *Assumptions.* Assumptions are declared only where the facts cannot derive them. The facts query
   809	    reports each one with the facts that rely on it, and any change to the declared set is reported
   810	    as an assumption diff that fails CI until it is recorded with the change (R-0589).
   811	  - *Tables* (owner, 2026-11-06: "ok do everything", after Claude, Codex, agy and Kimi chose unanimously). The sections are the typed, versioned `[policy]` and `[assumptions]` tables;
   812	    an unknown key or version is refused. Slices 7 and 19 extend them.
   813	- <a id="decided-project-os-sandboxing"></a>**OS-level sandboxing is outside the compiler's scope**
   814	  (owner, 2026-11-06: "shouldn't be even part of a compiler, that's something the user can decide"). OS-level sandboxing
   815	  (seccomp/landlock, Capsicum, pledge/unveil) is outside the compiler's scope; users may apply it
   816	  themselves. No task or option covers it.
   817	- <a id="decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06"></a>**Diagnostics are checker outputs** (owner,
   818	  2026-11-06: "a", option (a) on every question of the [proposal](archive/crucial-2026-11-06/DIAGNOSTICS.md), reviewed by
   819	  Codex, agy and Kimi). The checker returns a typed result, never a Bool, and every diagnostic is
   820	  one record rendered several ways. Rule IDs are the Lean rule's name plus that rule's own revision
   821	  (for example `StmtOk.let_.admitted@2`), bumped when the rule's meaning changes, including through
   822	  its dependencies, and checked; retired IDs stay in a registry (revised 2026-11-06 (owner, 2026-11-06: "ok do everything", after Claude, Codex, agy and Kimi chose unanimously), replacing
   823	  "plus the spec version"; R-0603); there are no numeric codes. Fixes are offered only after being rechecked and
   824	  labelled with their status; only a fix proved meaning-preserving by a lemma may be applied
   825	  automatically. There are no warnings: weaker findings are facts, and policies may refuse them.
   826	  Output is human text on a terminal and JSON lines otherwise, both from the one record, forced by
   827	  `--format=human|json`. The checker result type is fixed in A2i, before the freeze (R-0592); the
   828	  record, its JSON schema, goldens and the parser's diagnostics land in B0 (R-0593). It refines
   829	  R-0479.
   830	- <a id="decided-project-memory-2026-11-06"></a>**Heap facts and memory policies** (owner,
   831	  2026-11-06: "a", option (a) on every question of the [proposal](archive/crucial-2026-11-06/MEMORY.md), reviewed by Codex,
   832	  agy and Kimi). Each function reports, per allocator lineage, requested bytes, net change and peak
   833	  in logical bytes, and the allocator footprint where its contract defines one; each is a sound
   834	  bound with a status, or "not established". Bounds are polynomials with `max` over counts, lengths
   835	  and depths. Std states a growth upper bound for each container operation. Policies may guarantee
   836	  both logical bytes and footprint. Budgets are written only in `Concrete.toml` policies
   837	  ([row](#decided-project-concrete-toml)). "Allocation cannot fail" is a derived fact, established
   838	  only from an allocator success theorem. Stack stays a separate fact from target artifacts. The
   839	  byte-accounting instrumentation is fixed in A2i (R-0592); slice 0 proves its zero-heap facts
   840	  (R-0594); the analysis, its theorems and the policy entries come with the allocation slice
   841	  (R-0595), not in phase E.
   842	- <a id="decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06"></a>**Secrets, noninterference and constant time**
   843	  (owner, 2026-11-06: "a", option (a) on every question of the [proposal](archive/crucial-2026-11-06/INFOFLOW.md), reviewed
   844	  by Codex, agy and Kimi). Noninterference and constant time are two separate claims (authority
   845	  rule 14), each a theorem about the source semantics of every accepted program, holding at the
   846	  binary only under named backend premises. The surface is the type `Secret<T>`, with `classify`,
   847	  `declassify(x, "reason")`, `ct_select` and `ct_eq`; a `declassify` is traced in the facts like a
   848	  `#[may_not_terminate]` permission. Control flow on a secret is refused (write `ct_select`);
   849	  container lengths are public, and a secret length is refused. `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for
   850	  scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and wiped on destroy, which revises
   851	  [Q2.2](#q2-2-revision). The observer model is the baseline constant-time model (branches, exact
